Brief

    Yaz0 is a compression algorithm developed by Nintendo that has been in use
    in their games since the Nintendo 64. It is a variant of the LZ77
    compression algorithm (Lempel-Ziv 1977, see reference below), specifically
    a variant on the LZSS algorithm.

Header

    All Yaz0 files start with a 16-byte header.

        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |  offset  |    type    |             Description              |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |       0  |   char[4]  |  Magic constant ("Yaz0")             |
        |       4  |  uint32_t  |  Size of uncompressed data in bytes  |
        |       8  |  uint32_t  |  Alignment hint                      |
        |      12  |   char[4]  |  Unused                              |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+

    The first field in the header is a 4-byte magic constant, the string
    "Yaz0". Well-formed implementations should reject any data that does not
    start with these bytes.

    The second field in the header is the size of the uncompressed data in
    bytes, well-formed implementations will use this value to confirm that all
    bytes have been read or written during compression and decompression
    respectively.

    The alignment hint has no bearing to the algorithm, it is a hint used by
    applications to align the uncompressed data in memory. Well-formed
    implementations should just pass this value transiently.

    The last four bytes are currently unused by any implementation and are
    reserved for future use by Nintendo. Well-formed implementations should
    just pass this value transiently.

Blocks

    After the header comes zero or more blocks.

    Literals and pairs are stored in groups of eight called blocks. Every
    block is at least 1 byte and at most 25 bytes long. The first byte in
    every block is a bitmask called the code byte. The most significant bit in
    the code byte represents the first literal or pair, and the least
    significant bit the last literal or pair. A raised bit indicates a literal
    encoding, and a low bit indicates a length-distance encoding.

    Length-distance pairs can be encoded as either a 2-byte short encoding or a
    3-byte long encoding. Lengths and distances are stored as biased values to
    increase the efficiency of the compressed data.

    A short encoding stores the length in the high nibble of the first byte,
    and stores the distance in the low nibble of the first byte and second
    byte. A bias of 1 is added to the distance, and a bias of 2 is added to the
    length.

        d = 1 + ((b[0] & 0xF) << 8 | b[1])
        l = 2 + (b[0] >> 4)

    If the high nibble of the first byte is set to zero, the pair is encoded
    in three bytes instead. The length can be read from the third byte by
    adding a bias of 18 to it.

        d =  1 + ((b[0] & 0xF) << 8 | b[1])
        l = 18 + b[2]

    As such, the shortest distance that can be encoded is 1 byte, and the
    longest distance that can be encoded is 4096 bytes. The shortest length
    that can be encoded is 3 bytes, and the longest length is 273 bytes.

Compression Algorithm

    The following pseudocode is a reproduction of the Yaz0 sliding window
    compression algorithm.

        while input is not empty do
            if deferred then
                deferred := false
                d := dd
                l := dl
            else
                match := longest occurrence of input at window

                if match exists then
                    d := distance to start of match
                    l := length of match

                    match := longest occurrence of input at window + 1

                    if match exists and match length >= l + 2 then
                        deferred := true
                        dd := distance to start of match
                        dl := length of match
                        d := ld
                        l := 1
                    end
                else
                    d := 0
                    l := 1
                end
            end

            output (d, l)

            discard l + 1 chars from front of window
            s := pop l + 1 chars from front of input
            append s to back of window
        end

    The compression algorithm is a variation of LZSS. It replaces repeated
    occurrences of "strings" (bytes) found within a sliding window by encoding
    them as length-distance pairs. If no match occurs anywhere in the previous
    4096 bytes, the compressor will encode a literal value. To speed up
    compression at the cost of size, the decompressor can reduce the size of
    the window.

    Yaz0 also defers the selection of matches with a lazy evaluation mechanism.
    After a match of length L has been found, the algorithm will search for
    another match at the next input byte. If the found match is longer than the
    current match, the compressor will encode a literal now and encode the
    deferred match later. This deferred match is often called a lookahead match
    by other implementations.

Decompression Algorithm

    The following pseudocode is a reproduction of the Yaz0 decompression
    algorithm.

        while input is not empty do
            if rem is 0 then
                cb := read byte at input
                rem := 8
            end

            lit := cb & most significant bit

            if lit then
                b := read byte at input
                append b to output
            else
                b0 := read byte at input
                b1 := read byte at input

                d := 1 + ((b0 & 0xF) << 8 | b1)
                l := 2 + (b0 >> 4)

                if l is less than 3 then
                    b2 := read byte at input
                    l := b2 + 18
                end

                bytes := output[-d .. -d+l]
                append bytes to output
            end

            cb := cb << 1
            rem := rem - 1
        end

    Decompressing a Yaz0 encoded file is fast and trivial, making it very
    well-suited for use in video games to compress assets. While compression is
    very slow, decompression requires no state other than the last 4096 bytes
    of decoded data.

    The only pitfall in decompression that must be handled with care is that
    the last group can be a partial group. This is why decompression must stop
    immediately when the amount of bytes written to output is equal to the
    uncompressed size reported in the header.

Conclusion

